I looked at the article that you reference and found this statement in terms of what you should do.

Avoid using CSS @import and use other methods like link tags, inlining CSS, or combining CSS to allow the browser to load your page faster, and also vastly improve your visitors' page experience.

I then opened a fresh Backdrop sandbox site to view the page source.



I know that Backdrop CMS comes with an ability to aggregate and compress CSS files, which seems to be turned on by default. When I look at the page source, I'm seeing links to stylesheets rather than @import statements. I believe that any @import rules (if we have them) would be in the original css files before they are aggregated, compressed, and cached. So, it's not clear to me how @import rules would be or are currently slowing down Backdrop.

Can you give specific examples of where Backdrop is using @import in ways that might effect browser performance?


