@domaingood - Don't these @import links go away when you go to admin/config/development/performance and turn on "Aggregate and compress CSS files."

I believe that the best practice is to enable "aggregate and compress CSS file" on any production site. I assume that is in part to deal with this issue.

"Aggregate and Compress CSS files" is on by default for new sites (Since 1.5.0 - #2194).

Turning that setting off is largely to help during development so that it's easier to find css rules.

Truthfully, I'm not entirely certain if there is more going on here that I am not aware of.

So, is it important that we don't use "@import url" at all. Or should we be doing a better job of reminding users when Aggregation and Compression are turned off. I spun up a test site and disabled all the performance settings and didn't see any reminders or status page messages reminding me to turn them back on.


@stpaultim @jenlampton So when "Aggregate and Compress CSS files" on then we don't use "@import url" at all. can we remove import url.


@domaingood - I can't speak to the question of whether or not is would be possible to eliminate the use of @import completely.

This is what I can say. Backdrop provides two modes for handling css files.


Higher performance mode with "Aggregate and Compress CSS files" active. In this mode, we are not using @import (as far as I can see).

We can turn off "Aggregate and Compress CSS files" during development, which degrades performance but provides for developers with the ability to see their CSS in many different files and to locate and edit specific CSS rules much easier (because they are not aggregated and minimized). With this turned off Backdrop does use @import and loads many more files than when they are aggregated.


The article you referenced suggests two alternatives (for performance):

a) Using <link> tags instead of @import

b) Combining or inlining CSS

We offer users the option to do both of these through "Aggregate and Compress CSS files".

These are the questions that I don't know the answer to:


Is it possible to use  tag instead of @import in the development mode and still have the same control and access to details about CSS

How difficult would this be and what are the benefits - when users already have the opportunity to "Aggregate and Compress CSS"?


It seems like the primary goal of this request is to improve performance of Backdrop. But, we already have the option to get these benefits in core. The secondary goal might be to improve the performance of Backdrop in what is already recognized as the lower performance "development" mode (it's not officially called that).

Which brings me back to 1) is it possible to do this without loosing the current control and 2) even if it is, is it a priority?

(There is no harm in leaving this ticket open in case others have ideas, but I am not yet convinced that is a problem that really needs a solution. I could be convinced otherwise by people smarter than me. It seems to me like there are better opportunities for us to improve performance in core.)

